  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nHeat and humidity are the main weather concern for today, with\nwidespread heat indices in the 90s expected this afternoon. A \npool of dewpoints in the low 70s remain across much of the \nsouthern half of MN and western WI, however northerly winds and\noutflow from last night's storms will usher in lower dewpoints.\nThis will limit heat indices from getting too out of control, \nthough southern MN may hold onto enough of this moisture into \nthe afternoon to approach 100 degree heat indices. The urban \nheat island of the Twin Cities metro will be the most vulnerable\nto heat impacts, with afternoon heat indices hovering right \naround that lower threshold of 95F. At the same time, WBGT are \nforecasted to be around 84-85F in the metro as well, even with \nour lower sun angle this time of year. This reaches a threshold \nfor high risk of heat related illness and suggests limited \nexercise outside, especially for vulnerable populations. We have\nopted to go ahead with a metro- specific Heat Advisory despite \nthe uncertainty and borderline values. It will be hot today \nregardless of thresholds and given that it is early September, \nit felt worthwhile to give the extra messaging boost for this \nanomalous heat. Our most recent Heat Advisory for the metro \ndates back to July 27th.\n\nThe best chance for any storms today will be along the MN/IA\nborder, though the latest hi-res guidance continues to trend \ndry for the region.\n\nWe remain in a hot and stormy pattern with ridging in place\noverhead through early next week. Long range guidance \ncontinues to favor storms and appreciable rainfall over western\nMN and the Dakotas where better forcing from northeastward \nadvancing shortwave disturbances is expected. The SWODY2 and 3 \nboth depict this idea as well, with the marginal risks each day \nonly clipping a part of western MN. The best chance for \nwidespread precip will likely come some time Monday into Tuesday\nas a more organized shortwave breaks down the upper level \nridge. Winds will then increase out of the northwest and advect \nin more seasonable temperatures and drier air through the end of\nthe week.",
